A||||15||beer_stud_21||Taboo engine swaps......||||1||
Z||000000||beer_stud_21||03-14-2001||02:22 PM||||I don't know about the technicalities of doing a swap like this or if it is even technically possible but im curious.  In the most recent Hot Rod mag (its not a sweet ass key lime charger on the cover) there is an article about 12 second hot rods that get 20mpg.  One is a Civic with about 500hp.  The article says he toasts vettes, ferarris, etc.  But the thing that got me was he said its totally reliable just oil changes and plugs.  And with gas here in hawaii at $2.00 a gal for regular the 25 city/35 highway gas milage sounds great to me.. It would be cool to have a 500hp fiero, even if they were honda hp.  Well what do you guys think? <P>OK, now its time go try out my as-of-yet unused flame retardent flame war suit..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||03-14-2001||03:00 PM||||No idea if it's possible, but...why not??  Not much worse than slipping a Ford Taurus SHO in there (Bob Steiger??).  I want one of those Honda Indy (F1?) engines in mine.  Revs to something like 10 grand.  Wow!||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||03-14-2001||03:23 PM||||The S2000 engine redlines at like 9500rpms.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||03-14-2001||03:45 PM||||I'm a little skeptical about a 500-hp Civic being super-reliable.  I mean, if it were that easy to build a reliable 500-hp sub-2.0 liter street engine, why wouldn't every automaker offer one?<P>I can see Monkeyman's Fiero now, powered by a Penske Racing/Honda HR-1 powerplant.  2.65 liters, 900 hp, redlines at 16,500 rpm.  But where will he get the methanol to run it?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||03-14-2001||04:07 PM||||"Reliable" means somethign different to the guy who built it than it does to an auto manufacturer.<BR>A manufacturer wants a car they can sell and forget, and never have any warantee claims or customer dissatisfaction for 100,000 miles or more.<BR>What do you wager that civic engine cost to build? At that power level it would have to be turbo or super charged. I'd guess $12,000.<BR>The car didn't cost that much new.||||reg||1
Z||000005||stsmithgt||03-14-2001||04:43 PM||||Those FWD rockets feed on the bottle .<BR>Speedvision had a clip on those guys drag racing at a strip in Las Vegas.  Those cars are funny looking, but they're quick.||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||03-14-2001||04:49 PM||||a B18c1 with a Nitrous express plate kit makes 485 at the wheels.. on a STOCK motor.. the tranny, motor combo can be found for about $1600.. install and you'll have to get the $400 (new) wiring harness and the $250 computer from Python Remanufacturing. yeah it can be done and for about the same price as a TPI 350 with more torque... I see high HP imoprts everyday... but very few of them is really drivable.  <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>  <A HREF="http://www.enthuzacar.com" TARGET=_blank>www.enthuzacar.com</A>   - thats me on the weekends...||||reg||1
Z||000007||filthyscarecrow||03-15-2001||12:12 AM||||problem is that you'd have to use everything form the honda- the electronics and the gearbox (don't get me wrong, these are great gearboxes. each shift puts you right in the meatiest part of the powerband for the next gear). but with the gearbox you'd have to fabricate special drive shafts. i know some honda engines spin backwards in relation to american engines (and maybe the rest of the world). like i sed once before, a turbo B18C5 would put out about 250-275 extremely reliable HP, get 25-30mpg, and send a fiero to 60 in about 5 seconds. (the type R gearboxes that go with the B18C5 also come with a limited slip differential : )<BR>  but if i had the money, i would stuff that engine in the back seat of a civic hatchback ala the renault clio sport or ford shogun (ford festiva with a tore-ass SHO engine in it). no weight+lotsa power=a lot of fun and stopping for mr. ociffer becomes optional. ahh the dreams of a poor man...<P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||03-15-2001||02:23 AM||||Find me an old Zumalt kit and put a GM V8 in my Fiero or plop a nice 3800 SC back there! And I agree that 500 hp motors are yet to be proven reliable on everyday street driving even if half of the power is made with laughing gas...(of course the 500 hp Viper isn't on the market yet...Droool...)<P>All this talk about Maserattis, Yamaha SHO, and now Honda motors back there under the trunk lid are just making me sad! I will agree that Honda motors are just fantasticly engineered but I think they belong IN HONDAS (or Acuras..)! <P>I am certainly surprised that no one has wondered about the NSX motor in a Fiero...its already set up for midengine...hey why not an MR-2 motor while we are at it?<P>Jeeez!<P>Before I step off my soap box and jump for my asbestos underwear, I just want to say that for me, GM products need to stay GM, for better or worse, if for nothing else brand loyalty, an outdated quaint notion I realize but mine none the less.<P>Okay, bring it on! (EEEK!)||||reg||8
Z||000009||hugh||03-15-2001||07:16 AM||||Over 400hp is achievable with a 3.8 GN engine.Archie has had kits in the past for them.I think he said in a fairly recent post that if he had enough orders for them he would make some kits.The engine can even be made lighter than it already is,with the addition of aluminum heads.The plumbing would be a nightmare,but you can have an engine with over 400 reliable horsepower and still get over 20 miles a gallon.I get about 15 mpg /city and over 20mpg highway with 3.89 gears in my car,which weighs about 3700 lbs. with me in it.In a lighter car with higher gears the mileage could go up. substantially.||||reg||1
Z||000010||1FST2M6||03-15-2001||08:14 AM||||the skirts on the pistons on a c5 are to short and the pistons fail. the S/C kit the J/R sells for the car only put the car at 215 at the wheels... the F-max turbo kit make 280 and thats with water injection.. the c1 or a B16a1 would be much better. and from 86-99 civic/integra they all spin bass ackwards from the rest of the world.<P>i'd still shoot for a TPI 350... more tq and the  mileage your wanting...  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tigger||03-15-2001||09:17 AM||||I noticed on the Michigan Fiero Club messageboard someone mentioned something that they have a 426/7 hemi powered '84 Indy Fiero.<P>I question if it's real, anyone know about it?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||03-15-2001||09:30 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>the skirts on the pistons on a c5 are to short and the pistons fail. the S/C kit the J/R sells for the car only put the car at 215 at the wheels... the F-max turbo kit make 280 and thats with water injection.. the c1 or a B16a1 would be much better. and from 86-99 civic/integra they all spin bass ackwards from the rest of the world.<P>i'd still shoot for a TPI 350... more tq and the  mileage your wanting...  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Who cares if they spin backwards? What matters is that the axles turn the right direction!<BR>I would not use a any Fiero trans with a Honda engine. I don't think a Getrag would like being behind a 10,000 rpm B16.||||reg||1
